                "content": "**Notation**\n\n* \\(H_{4}Y\\) \u2013 free EDTA molecule  \n* \\(Y^{4-}\\) \u2013 fully de\u2011protonated EDTA ligand  \n* \\([Y']\\) \u2013 total concentration of all protonated/EDTA species **that are not bound to a metal**  \n* \\(\\alpha _{Y}= [Y^{4-}]/[Y']\\) \u2013 fraction of EDTA that is in the \\(Y^{4-}\\) form  \n\nAll activity coefficients are taken as unity (ideal behaviour).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Protonation fraction of EDTA  \n\nThe successive dissociation equilibria are  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathrm{H_4Y}&\\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{H_3Y^- }+ \\mathrm{H^+}&\\;K_{a1}\\\\\n\\mathrm{H_3Y^-}&\\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{H_2Y^{2-}}+ \\mathrm{H^+}&\\;K_{a2}\\\\\n\\mathrm{H_2Y^{2-}}&\\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{HY^{3-}}+ \\mathrm{H^+}&\\;K_{a3}\\\\\n\\mathrm{HY^{3-}}&\\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{Y^{4-}}+ \\mathrm{H^+}&\\;K_{a4}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAdding the four equilibria gives the overall reaction  \n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{H_4Y}\\rightleftharpoons \\mathrm{Y^{4-}}+4\\mathrm{H^+},\\qquad \nK=\\frac{K_{a1}K_{a2}K_{a3}K_{a4}}{[H^+]^{4}}\n\\]\n\nWriting the equilibria cumulatively and dividing by the total\nnon\u2011complexed EDTA concentration \\([Y']\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\displaystyle \n\\alpha _{Y}= \\frac{[Y^{4-}]}{[Y']}\n      =\\frac{K_{a1}K_{a2}K_{a3}K_{a4}}\n            {K_{a1}K_{a2}K_{a3}K_{a4}\n             +K_{a1}K_{a2}K_{a3}[H^+]\n             +K_{a1}K_{a2}[H^+]^{2}\n             +K_{a1}[H^+]^{3}\n             +[H^+]^{4}} }\n\\]\n\nAt pH\u202f=\u202f10\u2003(\\([H^+]=10^{-10}\\,\\text{M}\\))\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nK_{a1}&=10^{-2.00}=1.00\\times10^{-2}\\\\\nK_{a2}&=10^{-2.67}=2.15\\times10^{-3}\\\\\nK_{a3}&=10^{-6.16}=6.87\\times10^{-7}\\\\\nK_{a4}&=10^{-10.26}=5.48\\times10^{-11}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEvaluating the expression gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\alpha _{Y}=0.52\\;(3\\;\\text{s.f.})}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Conditional (metal\u2011free) formation constants  \n\nThe intrinsic formation constants (metal\u202f+\u202fligand) are  \n\n\\[\nK_{f}^{\\mathrm{Zn}} = 3.00\\times10^{16},\n\\qquad\nK_{f}^{\\mathrm{Mg}} = 5.00\\times10^{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause only the de\u2011protonated ligand binds appreciably,\n\n\\[\nK_{f}^{\\mathrm{M}}=\n\\frac{[\\mathrm{MY^{2-}}]}{[\\mathrm{M^{2+}}][Y^{4-}]}\n\\]\n\nThe total concentration of free (uncomplexed) EDTA is \\([Y']\\); therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{[\\mathrm{MY^{2-}}]}{[\\mathrm{M'}]}\n    =K_{f}^{\\mathrm{M}}\\;\\frac{[Y^{4-}]}{[Y']}\n    =K_{f}^{\\mathrm{M}}\\;\\alpha _{Y}\n\\]\n\nand the **conditional** constants defined in the question are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{K_{\\mathrm M}''=\n       \\frac{[\\mathrm{MY^{2-}}]}{[\\mathrm M'][Y']}}\n\\]\n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nK_{\\mathrm{Zn}}'' &=\\frac{K_{f}^{\\mathrm{Zn}}\\;\\alpha _{Y}}{\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}}}\n                  =\\frac{3.00\\times10^{16}\\times0.5195}{1.00\\times10^{5}}\n                  =1.56\\times10^{11}\\\\[4pt]\nK_{\\mathrm{Mg}}'' &=\\frac{K_{f}^{\\mathrm{Mg}}\\;\\alpha _{Y}}{\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}}}\n                  =\\frac{5.00\\times10^{8}\\times0.5195}{10}\n                  =2.60\\times10^{7}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(three significant figures)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Mass balances and reduction to a single equation  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\ny=[Y']\\quad (\\text{unknown})\n\\]\n\n**Metal balances**\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{\\mathrm{Zn}}&=[\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}](1+\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}})\n                 +[\\mathrm{ZnY^{2-}}]\\\\[2pt]\n               &=[\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}](1+\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}})\n                 +K_{\\mathrm{Zn}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}}[\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]\\,y \\\\[4pt]\n\\Rightarrow\\;\n[\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]&=\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{Zn}}}\n                        {(1+\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}})+K_{\\mathrm{Zn}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}}\\,y}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3\u20111}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_{\\mathrm{Mg}}&=[\\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}](1+\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}})\n                 +[\\mathrm{MgY^{2-}}]\\\\[2pt]\n               &=[\\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}](1+\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}})\n                 +K_{\\mathrm{Mg}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}}[\\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}]\\,y \\\\[4pt]\n\\Rightarrow\\;\n[\\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}]&=\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{Mg}}}\n                        {(1+\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}})+K_{\\mathrm{Mg}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}}\\,y}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3\u20112}\n\\]\n\n**EDTA balance**\n\n\\[\nC_{Y}=y+[\\mathrm{ZnY^{2-}}]+[\\mathrm{MgY^{2-}}]\n\\]\n\nUsing the expressions for the complexes (from the definitions of the\nconditional constants)\n\n\\[\n[\\mathrm{ZnY^{2-}}]=K_{\\mathrm{Zn}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}}[\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]\\,y,\n\\qquad\n[\\mathrm{MgY^{2-}}]=K_{\\mathrm{Mg}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}}[\\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}]\\,y\n\\]\n\nand substituting (3\u20111) and (3\u20112) gives a single equation containing only\n\\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\nC_{Y}=y\\Bigl[1+\n\\frac{K_{\\mathrm{Zn}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}}\\,C_{\\mathrm{Zn}}}\n     {(1+\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}})+K_{\\mathrm{Zn}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}}\\,y}\n+\n\\frac{K_{\\mathrm{Mg}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}}\\,C_{\\mathrm{Mg}}}\n     {(1+\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}})+K_{\\mathrm{Mg}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}}\\,y}\n\\Bigr]}\n\\tag{3\u20113}\n\\]\n\nAll constants are known; solving (3\u20113) for \\(y\\) provides the required\nconcentration of free (protonated) EDTA.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Numerical solution  \n\nInsert the numerical data  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}}&=1.00\\times10^{5}, &\n\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}}&=10\\\\[2pt]\nK_{\\mathrm{Zn}}''&=1.56\\times10^{11}, &\nK_{\\mathrm{Mg}}''&=2.60\\times10^{7}\\\\[2pt]\nC_{\\mathrm{Zn}}&=1.00\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{M},&\nC_{\\mathrm{Mg}}&=1.00\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{M}\\\\[2pt]\nC_{Y}&=1.20\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{M}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEquation (3\u20113) becomes  \n\n\\[\n1.20\\times10^{-3}=y\\Bigl[1+\n\\frac{1.56\\times10^{13}}\n     {1.00\\times10^{5}+1.56\\times10^{16}y}\n+\n\\frac{2.60\\times10^{5}}\n     {11+2.60\\times10^{8}y}\n\\Bigr]\n\\]\n\nSolving (e.g. by Newton\u2013Raphson or simple iteration) gives  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{y=[Y']=1.06\\times10^{-8}\\ \\text{M}}\n\\]\n\nNow the remaining quantities follow directly from the balance\nexpressions.\n\n*Denominators*\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nD_{\\mathrm{Zn}}&=(1+\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}})+K_{\\mathrm{Zn}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}}\\,y\n                =1.00001+1.56\\times10^{16}(1.06\\times10^{-8})\\\\\n               &=1.6518\\times10^{8}\\\\[4pt]\nD_{\\mathrm{Mg}}&=(1+\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}})+K_{\\mathrm{Mg}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}}\\,y\n                =11+2.60\\times10^{8}(1.06\\times10^{-8})\\\\\n               &=13.747\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Free metal ions*\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n[\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]&=\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{Zn}}}{D_{\\mathrm{Zn}}}\n                 =\\frac{1.00\\times10^{-3}}{1.6518\\times10^{8}}\n                 =\\mathbf{6.05\\times10^{-12}\\;M}\\\\[4pt]\n[\\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}]&=\\frac{C_{\\mathrm{Mg}}}{D_{\\mathrm{Mg}}}\n                 =\\frac{1.00\\times10^{-3}}{13.747}\n                 =\\mathbf{7.28\\times10^{-5}\\;M}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Uncomplexed metal species (the \u201cprime\u201d forms)*  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n[\\mathrm{Zn'}]&=\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}}[\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]\n               =1.00\\times10^{5}\\times6.05\\times10^{-12}\n               =\\mathbf{6.05\\times10^{-7}\\;M}\\\\[4pt]\n[\\mathrm{Mg'}]&=\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}}[\\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}]\n               =10\\times7.28\\times10^{-5}\n               =\\mathbf{7.28\\times10^{-4}\\;M}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n*Complexes*\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n[\\mathrm{ZnY^{2-}}]&=K_{\\mathrm{Zn}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Zn}}[\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]\\,y\\\\\n                   &=1.56\\times10^{11}\\times1.00\\times10^{5}\\times6.05\\times10^{-12}\\times1.06\\times10^{-8}\\\\\n                   &=\\mathbf{1.00\\times10^{-3}\\;M}\\\\[6pt]\n[\\mathrm{MgY^{2-}}]&=K_{\\mathrm{Mg}}''\\,\\alpha _{\\mathrm{Mg}}[\\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}]\\,y\\\\\n                   &=2.60\\times10^{7}\\times10\\times7.28\\times10^{-5}\\times1.06\\times10^{-8}\\\\\n                   &=\\mathbf{2.01\\times10^{-4}\\;M}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nA quick check of the EDTA balance:\n\n\\[\ny+[\\mathrm{ZnY^{2-}}]+[\\mathrm{MgY^{2-}}]\n=1.06\\times10^{-8}+1.00\\times10^{-3}+2.01\\times10^{-4}\n=1.21\\times10^{-3}\\ \\text{M}\\approx C_{Y}\n\\]\n\n(The slight excess is due to rounding.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  p\u2011values  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\np\\mathrm{Zn}&=-\\log_{10}[\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]\n            =-\\log_{10}(6.05\\times10^{-12})\n            =\\mathbf{11.2}\\\\[4pt]\np\\mathrm{Mg}&=-\\log_{10}[\\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}]\n            =-\\log_{10}(7.28\\times10^{-5})\n            =\\mathbf{4.14}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n(three significant figures)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Equilibrium constant for the exchange reaction  \n\n\\[\n\\mathrm{Zn' + MgY^{2-}\\;\\rightleftharpoons\\; ZnY^{2-}+ Mg'}\n\\]\n\nThe equilibrium expression is  \n\n\\[\nK_{\\text{ex}}=\n\\frac{[\\mathrm{ZnY^{2-}}]/[\\mathrm{Zn'}]}\n     {[\\mathrm{MgY^{2-}}]/[\\mathrm{Mg'}]}\n   =\\frac{K_{\\mathrm{Zn}}''[Y']}{K_{\\mathrm{Mg}}''[Y']}\n   =K_{\\mathrm{Zn}}''K_{\\mathrm{Mg}''}\\,[Y']^{2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\([Y']\\) is the total concentration of free EDTA.  \nWith \\([Y']\\approx C_{Y}=1.20\\times10^{-3}\\,\\text{M}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nK_{\\text{ex}}&=\n(1.56\\times10^{11})(2.60\\times10^{7})(1.20\\times10^{-3})^{2}\\\\\n&=5.84\\times10^{12}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{K_{\\text{ex}}\\approx5.8\\times10^{12}}\n\\]\n\n**Why a huge \\(K_{\\text{ex}}\\) does **not** force \\([\\mathrm{MgY^{2-}}]=0\\)**  \n\nThe constant compares *ratios* of activities of reactants and products.\nEven if the ratio is enormous, the absolute concentrations of the\nreactants may be vanishingly small.  \nIn the present mixture the free Zn species \\([\\mathrm{Zn'}]\\) is\n\\(6.0\\times10^{-7}\\,\\text{M}\\); consequently the forward reaction cannot\nconsume more MgY than is supplied by the tiny amount of Zn'.  \nMost of the Mg stays free (or loosely bound to the competing ligand)\nbecause its own conditional constant (\\(2.6\\times10^{7}\\)) is far smaller\nthan that for Zn (\\(1.6\\times10^{11}\\)). Thus a very large exchange\nconstant merely tells us that whenever both Zn\u2019 and MgY are present the\nreaction proceeds essentially to completion, but it does **not** imply\nthat MgY disappears altogether when its initial concentration is already\nlow.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Summary of numerical results (three significant figures)**  \n\n| Quantity | Value |\n|----------|-------|\n| \\(\\alpha _{Y}\\) | 0.520 |\n| \\(K_{\\mathrm{Zn}}''\\) | \\(1.56\\times10^{11}\\) |\n| \\(K_{\\mathrm{Mg}}''\\) | \\(2.60\\times10^{7}\\) |\n| \\([Y']\\) (=\u202f\\(y\\)) | \\(1.06\\times10^{-8}\\)\u202fM |\n| \\([\\mathrm{ZnY^{2-}}]\\) | \\(1.00\\times10^{-3}\\)\u202fM |\n| \\([\\mathrm{MgY^{2-}}]\\) | \\(2.01\\times10^{-4}\\)\u202fM |\n| \\([\\mathrm{Zn'}]\\) | \\(6.05\\times10^{-7}\\)\u202fM |\n| \\([\\mathrm{Mg'}]\\) | \\(7.28\\times10^{-4}\\)\u202fM |\n| \\([\\mathrm{Zn^{2+}}]\\) | \\(6.05\\times10^{-12}\\)\u202fM |\n| \\([\\mathrm{Mg^{2+}}]\\) | \\(7.28\\times10^{-5}\\)\u202fM |\n| \\(p\\mathrm{Zn}\\) | 11.2 |\n| \\(p\\mathrm{Mg}\\) | 4.14 |\n| \\(K_{\\text{ex}}\\) (exchange) | \\(5.8\\times10^{12}\\) |\n\nThese values satisfy all mass\u2011balance constraints and illustrate the\nstrong preferential binding of Zn\u00b2\u207a by EDTA compared with Mg\u00b2\u207a.",
                "reasoning_content": "We need to answer each subquestion with derivations and calculations.\n\nFirst, define EDTA protonation equilibria:\n\nH4Y \u21cc H3Y^- + H+   Ka1\nH3Y^- \u21cc H2Y^2- + H+   Ka2\nH2Y^2- \u21cc HY^3- + H+   Ka3\nHY^3- \u21cc Y^4- + H+   Ka4\n\nTotal EDTA species sum = [Y'] = sum over all species.\n\nAlpha_Y = [Y^4-]/[Y']; we can express using successive equilibria: \u03b1_Y = (Ka1 Ka2 Ka3 Ka4) / (Ka1 Ka2 Ka3 Ka4 + Ka1 Ka2 Ka3 [H+] + ... etc). Actually general formula: fraction of fully deprotonated = Ka1 Ka2 Ka3 Ka4 / (Ka1 Ka2 Ka3 Ka4 + Ka1 Ka2 Ka3 [H+] + Ka1 Ka2 [H+]^2 + Ka1 [H+]^3 + [H+]^4). Because each step adds one H+. Equivalent.\n\nCompute numeric at pH 10 => [H+] = 1e-10.\n\nCompute Ka values: Ka1 = 10^(-2)=0.01; Ka2=10^(-2.67)=~2.15e-3; Ka3=10^(-6.16)=6.87e-7; Ka4=10^(-10.26)=5.48e-11.\n\nNow compute denominator D = Ka1 Ka2 Ka3 Ka4 + Ka1 Ka2 Ka3 [H+] + Ka1 Ka2 [H+]^2 + Ka1 [H+]^3 + [H+]^4.\n\nCalculate product P = Ka1*Ka2*Ka3*Ka4.\n\nLet's compute stepwise.\n\nKa1=0.01\nKa2\u22482.15443469e-3 (since 10^-2.67)\nKa3\u22486.867e-7 (10^-6.16)\nKa4\u22485.48e-11 (10^-10.26)\n\nP = 0.01 * 2.154e-3 = 2.154e-5\nTimes Ka3: 2.154e-5 * 6.867e-7 \u2248 1.479e-11\nTimes Ka4: 1.479e-11 * 5.48e-11 \u2248 8.09e-22. Very tiny.\n\nTerm2 = Ka1 Ka2 Ka3 [H+] = (0.01*2.154e-3*6.867e-7)*1e-10 = same product without Ka4 times 1e-10 = 1.479e-11 *1e-10 =1.479e-21.\n\nTerm3 = Ka1 Ka2 [H+]^2 = 0.01*2.154e-3*(1e-20) = 2.154e-5 *1e-20 =2.154e-25.\n\nTerm4 = Ka1 [H+]^3 = 0.01*1e-30 =1e-32.\n\nTerm5 = [H+]^4 =1e-40.\n\nDenominator ~ sum dominated by term2 (largest) =1.479e-21 vs term1 8e-22 smaller. So denominator \u22481.557e-21.\n\n\u03b1_Y = P/D = 8.09e-22 / 1.557e-21 = 0.5195 approx. Wait compute ratio: 8.09e-22 /1.557e-21 =0.5195 indeed about 0.52. That seems high; at pH 10, many EDTA molecules are fully deprotonated? Yes because last pKa is 10.26, near pH 10, about half deprotonated. Good.\n\nThus \u03b1_Y \u2248 0.52.\n\nNow conditional formation constants:\n\nK_Zn'' = ([ZnY^2-])/([Zn'][Y']) = K_f^{Zn} * \u03b1_Y . Since [ZnY^2-] = K_f^{Zn} [Zn^{2+}] [Y^4-]; divide numerator and denominator: [Zn'][Y'] = [Zn^{2+}] ([Y']+?) Wait definition: \u03b1_Zn = [Zn']/[Zn^{2+}] = 1e5. So [Zn'] = \u03b1_Zn [Zn^{2+}]. Similarly [Y'] = total EDTA. Then [ZnY^2-] = K_f^{Zn} [Zn^{2+}] [Y^4-] = K_f^{Zn} [Zn^{2+}] \u03b1_Y [Y']. Plug into ratio: K_Zn'' = (K_f^{Zn} [Zn^{2+}] \u03b1_Y [Y']) / (\u03b1_Zn [Zn^{2+}] [Y']) = K_f^{Zn} \u03b1_Y / \u03b1_Zn.\n\nSimilarly K_Mg'' = K_f^{Mg} \u03b1_Y / \u03b1_Mg.\n\nCompute numbers.\n\nK_f_Zn = 3.00e16 ; \u03b1_Y=0.5195 ; \u03b1_Zn=1e5.\n\n=> K_Zn'' = 3e16 *0.5195 /1e5 = 3e16 *0.005195 = 1.5585e14. Let's calculate: 0.5195/1e5 =5.195e-6. Multiply by 3e16 gives 1.5585e11? Wait 3e16 *5.195e-6 = 1.5585e11. Check: 3e16 *5e-6 =1.5e11. Yes earlier miscalc. So K_Zn'' \u2248 1.56\u00d710^{11}.\n\nNow K_Mg'': K_f_Mg=5e8 ; \u03b1_Y=0.5195 ; \u03b1_Mg=10.\n\n=> factor \u03b1_Y/\u03b1_Mg =0.5195/10 =0.05195.\n\nMultiply: 5e8 *0.05195 = 2.5975e7 \u2248 2.60\u00d710^7.\n\nThus conditional constants.\n\nNow (3): Let y = [Y']. Total EDTA concentration C_Y = 1.20e-3 = [Y'] + [ZnY^2-] + [MgY^2-]? Actually there are also possibly other protonated species, but they are accounted in [Y']; we have already defined \u03b1_Y fraction fully deprotonated. However total EDTA includes all protonation states plus complexes? Usually total EDTA = [Y'] + [ZnY^2-] + [MgY^2-]. But [Y'] already includes all non-complexed species (including various protonation states). So yes.\n\nMass balance for metals:\n\nC_Zn = [Zn^{2+}] + [Zn'] + [ZnY^2-].\n\nBut [Zn'] = \u03b1_Zn [Zn^{2+}].\n\nSo C_Zn = [Zn^{2+}] (1 + \u03b1_Zn) + [ZnY^2-].\n\nSimilarly for Mg: C_Mg = [Mg^{2+}] (1 + \u03b1_Mg) + [MgY^2-].\n\nComplex concentrations expressed via conditional constants:\n\n[ZnY^2-] = K_Zn'' [Zn'] [Y'] = K_Zn'' \u03b1_Zn [Zn^{2+}] y.\n\nAlternatively use K_Zn'' = [ZnY^2-]/([Zn'] y).\n\nSimilarly [MgY^2-] = K_Mg'' [Mg'] y = K_Mg'' \u03b1_Mg [Mg^{2+}] y.\n\nNow substitute [Zn^{2+}] from Zn balance: [Zn^{2+}] = C_Zn / (1+\u03b1_Zn + K_Zn'' \u03b1_Zn y). Wait careful: C_Zn = [Zn^{2+}] (1+\u03b1_Zn) + K_Zn'' \u03b1_Zn [Zn^{2+}] y = [Zn^{2+}] [ (1+\u03b1_Zn) + K_Zn'' \u03b1_Zn y ].\n\nThus [Zn^{2+}] = C_Zn / D_Zn where D_Zn = (1+\u03b1_Zn) + K_Zn'' \u03b1_Zn y.\n\nSimilarly [Mg^{2+}] = C_Mg / D_Mg where D_Mg = (1+\u03b1_Mg) + K_Mg'' \u03b1_Mg y.\n\nNow we also have EDTA balance: C_Y = y + [ZnY^2-] + [MgY^2-] = y + K_Zn'' \u03b1_Zn [Zn^{2+}] y + K_Mg'' \u03b1_Mg [Mg^{2+}] y.\n\nFactor y: C_Y = y [ 1 + K_Zn'' \u03b1_Zn [Zn^{2+}] + K_Mg'' \u03b1_Mg [Mg^{2+}] ].\n\nReplace [Zn^{2+}], [Mg^{2+}] expressions.\n\nPlug them: [Zn^{2+}] = C_Zn / D_Zn ; [Mg^{2+}] = C_Mg / D_Mg.\n\nThus C_Y = y [ 1 + K_Zn'' \u03b1_Zn (C_Zn / D_Zn) + K_Mg'' \u03b1_Mg (C_Mg / D_Mg) ].\n\nNow D_Zn = (1+\u03b1_Zn) + K_Zn'' \u03b1_Zn y.\n\nD_Mg = (1+\u03b1_Mg) + K_Mg'' \u03b1_Mg y.\n\nAll constants known. This yields equation solely in y.\n\nSimplify maybe numeric solve.\n\nGiven values: \u03b1_Zn=1e5, \u03b1_Mg=10.\n\nK_Zn''\u22481.5585e11 ; K_Mg''\u22482.5975e7.\n\nC_Zn=C_Mg=1e-3.\n\nNow compute terms.\n\nLet\u2019s compute D_Zn = (1+1e5)+1.5585e11 *1e5 * y = 100001 + 1.5585e16 y.\n\nBecause K_Zn'' \u03b1_Zn = 1.5585e11 *1e5 =1.5585e16.\n\nSimilarly D_Mg = (1+10)+2.5975e7 *10 * y = 11 + 2.5975e8 y.\n\nNow compute term A = K_Zn'' \u03b1_Zn C_Zn / D_Zn = (1.5585e16 *1e-3)/ D_Zn = 1.5585e13 / D_Zn.\n\nTerm B = K_Mg'' \u03b1_Mg C_Mg / D_Mg = (2.5975e8 *10 *1e-3)/ D_Mg = 2.5975e6 / D_Mg.\n\nEquation: C_Y = y[1 + A + B] with C_Y=1.20e-3.\n\nThus 1.20e-3 = y[1 + 1.5585e13 / (100001 + 1.5585e16 y) + 2.5975e6 / (11 + 2.5975e8 y)].\n\nWe can attempt solving numerically. Expect y likely close to C_Y because most EDTA remains uncomplexed? But strong binding to Zn may consume much.\n\nCheck magnitude: If y small, denominators dominated by constants (100001, 11). Then A \u2248 1.5585e13 /100001 \u2248 1.558e8. That's huge >1, making RHS huge unless y extremely small. So likely y will be such that denominators become large due to term proportional to y.\n\nLet's solve iteratively.\n\nSuppose y is around 1e-4? Then D_Zn \u2248 1e5 + 1.5585e16 *1e-4 = 1e5 + 1.5585e12 \u2248 1.5585e12 (dominant). So D_Zn \u22481.5585e12. Then A \u2248 1.5585e13 /1.5585e12 =10. So A\u224810.\n\nSimilarly D_Mg \u2248 11 + 2.5975e8 *1e-4 = 11 + 2.5975e4 = 2.5986e4. Then B\u22482.5975e6 /2.5986e4 \u224899.96 \u2248100.\n\nThus RHS bracket \u22481+10+100=111. So 1.20e-3 = y *111 \u2192 y \u22481.08e-5. That's much lower than initial guess. Let's test consistency: With y=1.08e-5, recompute D's.\n\nD_Zn = 100001 + 1.5585e16 *1.08e-5 = 100001 + 1.68e11 \u2248 1.68e11 (dominant). So A = 1.5585e13 /1.68e11 = 92.8.\n\nD_Mg = 11 + 2.5975e8 *1.08e-5 = 11 + 2.807e3 = 2.818e3. B = 2.5975e6 /2.818e3 = 922.5.\n\nBracket = 1+92.8+922.5=1016.3. Then y = 1.20e-3 /1016.3 = 1.18e-6. Hmm lower.\n\nIterate again with y=1.18e-6.\n\nD_Zn = 100001 + 1.5585e16 *1.18e-6 = 100001 + 1.84e10 = 1.84e10 (dominant). A = 1.5585e13 /1.84e10 = 849.6.\n\nD_Mg = 11 + 2.5975e8 *1.18e-6 = 11 + 3.074e2 = 318.4. B = 2.5975e6 /318.4 = 8159.\n\nBracket = 1+849.6+8159=9010. Then y = 1.20e-3 /9010 = 1.33e-7.\n\nNext iteration y=1.33e-7.\n\nD_Zn = 100001 + 1.5585e16 *1.33e-7 = 100001 + 2.07e9 = 2.07e9. A = 1.5585e13 /2.07e9 = 7535.\n\nD_Mg = 11 + 2.5975e8 *1.33e-7 = 11 + 34.5 = 45.5. B = 2.5975e6 /45.5 = 57100.\n\nBracket = 1+7535+57100=64636. y = 1.20e-3 /64636 = 1.86e-8.\n\nNext y=1.86e-8.\n\nD_Zn = 100001 + 1.5585e16 *1.86e-8 = 100001 + 2.89e8 = 2.89e8. A = 1.5585e13 /2.89e8 = 53.9? Wait compute: 1.5585e13 /2.89e8 = 5.39e4 (53900). Actually 1.5585e13 /2.89e8 = 5.39e4.\n\nD_Mg = 11 + 2.5975e8 *1.86e-8 = 11 + 4849 = 4860. B = 2.5975e6 /4860 = 534.5.\n\nBracket = 1+53900+534.5=54435.5. y = 1.20e-3 /54435.5 = 2.20e-8.\n\nNow y increased slightly from 1.86e-8 to 2.20e-8. Converging perhaps around 2e-8.\n\nLet's try y=2.2e-8.\n\nD_Zn = 100001 + 1.5585e16 *2.2e-8 = 100001 + 3.43e8 = 3.